NAME
    sort - sort lines of text

SYNOPSIS
    <input> | sort [-r] [-n] [-u] [-f]

DESCRIPTION
    Reads all of standard input, sorts its lines, and writes them in order.
    A pipeline filter. Unlike the streaming filters, sort must buffer the
    whole input before it can emit anything, so it holds the input in its
    heap and sorts an in-place line index (heapsort).

    -r  reverse (descending) order
    -n  numeric: compare a leading integer on each line (ties broken
        lexicographically); a line with no number sorts as 0
    -u  unique: drop lines that compare equal to the previous emitted line
    -f  fold case (ASCII) when comparing

    Flags may be combined, e.g. -rn. The default is a plain byte
    (lexicographic) ascending sort.

LIMITS
    Bounded to the program's heap: a very large input is truncated at the
    cap, sorted, and emitted, with a one-line warning on the console (never
    into the sorted output).

EXAMPLES
    cat names.txt | sort
    cat names.txt | sort -u
    ps | sort
    cat sizes.txt | sort -rn

SEE ALSO
    uniq, grep, head, tail, wc
